fields.Core Themes JSON:
[
  {
    "title": "From Qigong Class To Cosmos",
    "body": "Chan traces her path to age four, when she watched a chi master ease her father's back pain in one session and reduce her sister's swollen ankle after doctor upon doctor had failed. She left a short software career and entered Chinese medicine school in 2003, and in an advanced qigong class outside of school dropped so deep into meditation that she experienced herself as the size of the cosmos before landing back in tears. She describes two or three years of dark night afterward, reading thousands of books on metaphysics when the internet was barely a resource, with the felt sense of closer to truth as her guide."
  },
  {
    "title": "An Inner Compass Of Energy",
    "body": "Chan says her classes are not scored on getting colors right; facilitators ask children what the knowing felt like, whether they were overthinking or second guessing, and they treat students who consistently name the next color in the pile as practicing precognition. One child told his family the GPS was wrong about the traffic ahead. Garton offers the adult version, asking whether a regular activity leaves you drained or charged, and describing how canceling a dreaded obligation lifts his energy and brings synchronicities. Chan warns that awakening can detour into nihilism, where reality is an illusion and nothing matters."
  },
  {
    "title": "Blindfold Vision Goes Online",
    "body": "Chan credits pioneers of mindsight from the 1980s and 1990s, then a 2011 meditation workshop with Drunvalo Melchizedek where she first heard of a group in Mexico training children in extraocular vision, presented then as something only a special few could learn. In 2020 Carolyn Corey's documentary Superhuman featured such children, and Corey connected with a Toronto teacher named Bodan, trained in a method called Info Vision, who tested whether kids could be trained over the internet. Chan contrasts that with the older model, where parents dropped a child off for ten or twelve sessions and were told to leave the building."
  },
  {
    "title": "Sixth Sense As First Sense",
    "body": "Chan describes working with Dalia, who trains blind adults to see using mindsight and who had already cultivated touching, hovering, hearing, smelling and tasting through qigong before ever wearing a blindfold, then saw behind her head before she saw in front of it. Their adult trainings now scaffold those other senses first and open visual mindsight on the last day, under a tagline about developing your sixth sense as your first sense. Garton argues that a brain assembling signals arriving from toes, ears and eyes at different speeds is inefficient, and that one unified sense being divided would explain synesthesia."
  },
  {
    "title": "A Parallel Education Revolution",
    "body": "Out of the shutdown years Chan built a project called Luminous Education Revolution, gathering parents she describes as biofield tuning practitioners, reiki masters, coaches and professional remote viewers, then redesigned the program with Bodan into six weeks: one week training parents as co-facilitators, four weeks of joint classes with games at home in between, and a closing week of debriefing, laughter and tears. Hundreds of families have come through, an Innovators Club invites advanced kids to invent new methods, and the CUSA nonprofit founded by Dr. Tom Campbell chose to collaborate after surveying groups worldwide for a year and a half."
  }
]

